# Optional, deliberately narrow WAF snippet for one application site.
#
# Copy the rules you need inside that site's block, before `php_server`, and
# test its normal login, checkout, API and plugin traffic before enabling it.
# This is a low-cost safety net, not a replacement for a maintained WAF/CDN.
# Every matcher below is independent: one match is enough to return 403.

# Block common source and backup-file probes.
@waf-sensitive-extension path_regexp waf_sensitive_extension (?i)\.(git|svn|env|htaccess|htpasswd|bak|sql|log|conf)$
respond @waf-sensitive-extension 403

@waf-webshell-path path_regexp waf_webshell_path (?i)(shell|c99|r57|hacktools?|wso|webshell)
respond @waf-webshell-path 403

# Block a small set of reconnaissance tools. Do not block curl, Python or
# generic browser strings: legitimate integrations use them.
@waf-scanner header_regexp waf_scanner User-Agent (?i)(sqlmap|nikto|masscan|zmeu|morfeus|jaascois|paros|nessus|openvas)
respond @waf-scanner 403

# Query-string probes. This is a single CEL matcher so its alternatives are
# OR'ed; the former multi-query_regexp example was neither portable Caddy nor
# semantically correct.
@waf-query expression `{query}.matches('(?i)(union.*select|select.*from|insert.*into|drop.*table|eval\\(|base64_decode|system\\(|exec\\(|\\.\\./|\\.%2f|%2e%2e/|onmouseover|onerror|onload|javascript:)')`
respond @waf-query 403
